**Verdict: APPROVE-WITH-NITS.**

Read-only. I reviewed `git diff 268c4f4b..59479d9d` (one commit, 9 files), read the plan (sections 1.2–1.10, slice C8), the BINDING architect rulings, the engineer's findings including the "Rebase and ruling amendments" section (which I treated as superseding), UCF 7.2.1, 7.7.2, 7.7.5–7.7.8, 7.9, 7.11.1 clauses 4 and 9, linker-dht 14.2.3 step 6, the final `ledger.cljc`, `grant.cljc`, `completion.cljc`, both new test namespaces in full, the grant/reclaim/admission/input test diffs, and `checkpoint.cljc` with its fixtures. No suite was run, per the brief (JVM/Node/Dart lanes and kondo are green on this exact tree); I re-checked the determinism guards myself. No file was edited.

## 1. Mutation pass by inspection — every guarantee located

**A release completes only with resumed evidence and a verified successor.** `report!` (completion.cljc:147-175) admits only the holder's exact 7.7.2 report shape (`:malformed-report` otherwise); `report-decision` (121-144) requires a known lease, the right occurrence, the lease's holder as author, liveness, no prior report (`:report-conflict`), no quarantine. `successor-defect` (94-113) enforces bytes-matching-the-address, version 1 and root role via `checkpoint/inspect` (hash mismatch → `:uninspectable`), origin naming exactly this occurrence and lease (`:wrong-origin`), same arbitration identity (`:foreign-arbitration`), an occurrence never seen — offered or any closure's successor (`:seen-occurrence`, `successor-seen?` ledger.cljc:455-462) — and `:yin.k/next-op-seq` not below the predecessor's baseline (`:counter-regression`). A halted body takes only the origin check (101-104), per ruling 1.

**One transaction.** `lapse-decision` (grant.cljc:184-207) threads `completion/closure` between the lapse and the epoch change; `closure` (completion.cljc:182-204) completes only on cause `:release`, a live reported lease on the occurrence's current lease, unquarantined, non-exhausting (`epoch ≠ max-epoch`), successor still unseen — else `[]`, a plain reclaim. Every refusal writes nothing (asserted against `@frames` in the tests).

**Fold fails closed** (ledger.cljc:465-533, 689-715, 745-747): report — `:malformed-fact`/`:unknown-lease`/`:wrong-occurrence`/`:duplicate-report` (keyed on `:yin.k/result`, per ruling)/`:ended-lease`/`:seen-occurrence`; closure — `:unreleased` requires the `:release` lapse earlier in the *same record* via `::unepoched` (a later record, another cause, or a position after the epoch change all refuse), `:unreported` on `:yin.k/result`, `:exhausted-occurrence`, `:closed-occurrence`, `:quarantined-occurrence`; edge — exactly one of successor/result (`:malformed-fact`), `:unpaired-edge` (no closure earlier in the record, or a second edge), `:successor-mismatch` (result must equal the report's; a successor edge for a reported result and vice versa both refuse), `:seen-occurrence`; post-record `:closure-without-edge`. A report by a non-holder is refused at `report!` (`:not-holder`); the recorded fact carries no author, and the fold trusting its own journal here is the C5 gate's settled rule — consistent.

**Chain invariants.** Acyclic by induction: every ancestor is in `:occurrences` or a closed successor, so `successor-seen?` refuses any back-edge at report and edge time (`a-cycle-is-refused`, `the-fold-keeps-the-chain-acyclic`). One successor per predecessor: an occurrence closes once (`:closed-occurrence`; `::unedged`); one predecessor per successor: the first edge makes S seen for every later edge and report (tested both at the authority and in the fold). A closed occurrence never grants: fold `grant-defect` (ledger.cljc:299), writer `grant-decision` (grant.cljc:175), hook (grant.cljc:313) — the latter two also asserted through the judge. Exhausted accepts no completion (completion.cljc:200; ledger.cljc:502; tested at `::authority/max-epoch 1` and at the real 2^52-1). Quarantined cannot complete: `closure` refuses (completion.cljc:199) and the fold refuses (ledger.cljc:504-505) — ruling 4, both tested.

**Successor offers** (`offer-refusal`, completion.cljc:211-234): a body with no origin is unconstrained; `:awaiting-completion` while the origin's lease is the predecessor's live lease; `:orphan` after any non-completing end (reclaim of another cause, exhaustion, quarantine, a closing lease or successor other than this body's, an address other than the reported one) and for a predecessor this ledger never saw (ruling 3). Because the offer must present bytes hashing to the reported address, the eligible successor is byte-identical to the verified body. A refused offer is never in `:occurrences`, so never grantable.

**Crash cuts and reopen.** All four cuts tested with journal cuts (`before-frame`/`after-frame-before-visible`/`torn-frame`) and `grant/reopen!`: exactly one lapse of the lease across cuts, closure and edge persist together or not at all (counted facts), reopen replays, nothing live is reclaimed after closure, the last checkpoint regrants before closure, and the successor is eligible exactly once after. **The C6 gate's C8 extension is done**: `rebuild-judge` puts `:dao.lease/released` in `:seen` for a lapse of cause `:release` (grant.cljc:411-414), tested through the judge with a reopen (completion_test.cljc:618-623) — Q6 confirmed built.

## 2. Merge fidelity (C6, C7, C10)

The diff against master over `ledger.cljc` and `grant.cljc` is strictly additive plus two intended rewrites: `lapse-decision`'s facts threading (grant.cljc:201-206, behavior-identical when `closure` answers `[]`) and `rebuild-judge`'s `:released` clause with its docstring rewording. Every C6/C7/C10 element survives exactly once: the rejected/refused pair (arms at ledger.cljc:641-656, attribute-order 127-128, `refused-defect` 364-374), fenced/quarantine (`fenced-defect`/`quarantine-defect` 393-425, arms 660-675), input (`input-defect` 429-448, arm 678-687), the `:rejection-without-refusal` post-record check (742-744) ahead of the new `:closure-without-edge`, and the `dissoc` covering all four transient keys (748). The fold arms, defect functions and attribute-order entries each appear once — nothing duplicated, nothing lost. The upstream test suites pass on this tree per the brief, and the offer-fixture swaps in `reclaim_test`/`admission_test`/`input_test` (successor → first-park) are the ruling-3 consequence, preserving each test's subject; `grant_test` keeps the `successor` fixture where its baseline matters via the new `succeeded` helper, which completes `fx/predecessor` into it under lease-7 — exactly the fixture's own origin.

## 3. The halted completion (ruling 1)

Wrong origin or wrong lease commits nothing (`a-halted-body-must-name-this-occurrence-and-lease`, frames compared). It cannot be double-reported (`:duplicate-report` on the same address, `:report-conflict` on the other kind, both directions tested) and the completion cannot be replayed (the recorded release answers ok as a replay, writes nothing — asserted). The chain's end is well-defined: `:yin.k/closed` carries `:yin.k/result` and no `:yin.k/successor`, so any later body naming the closed occurrence fails the successor comparison and is `:orphan` (asserted), and the halted body itself is `:not-offerable`. The terminal edge is the `succeeded` kind with `:yin.k/result` (attribute-order `[custody occurrence successor result]`, per the ruling), exactly-one-of enforced at ledger.cljc:520-524. The report at the epoch bound is recorded; its release exhausts without completing (ruling test 5, done).

## 4. Scope and guards

No inherited-id scope (C9 — nothing built; `:yin.k/closed` and the public `successor-seen?` are its inputs), no remote front (C11 — `report!` takes the composition's attribution as `author`, deviation 8, accepted), no `yin.vm` engine namespace (`completion.cljc` requires only `dao.jing.cbor` and ucf namespaces). Cross-host determinism: no float literals, no `#?(:clj …)` JVM-only tests in the new code, entity-ordered test helpers, order-independent boolean scans; I re-checked ≤80 columns and ASCII in the touched files myself. The C3 digest fixture is untouched (`authority_test.cljc` is not in the diff). Cross-slice probe: C7's admission cannot quarantine a closed occurrence — the tenure check answers `:stale` first (admission.cljc:176-183) — so no new hole opens between the slices.

## Findings

1. **`:stale` on a closed occurrence omits `:yin.k/closed true`** — `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/authority/admission.cljc:178-183`. Severity: **minor** (no safety loss — a driver's action on `:stale` is the same — but UCF 7.9 amendment r5 publishes the key: "`:stale` carries … `:yin.k/closed true` when the occurrence is closed", and 7.11.1 clause 8's "closed occurrence" fixture, untestable before C8 because nothing could close an occurrence, is now runnable and fails on it). This is C7 surface that C8's landing makes reachable for the first time; `admission.cljc` is untouched by this diff, so nothing was lost — the debt surfaced. Fix: extend the `cond->` at admission.cljc:179-182 to assoc `:yin.k/closed true` when `(:yin.k/closed occ)`, add the clause-8 fixture assertion; fits C9's admission touch or a one-line follow-up commit before C9. Does not block C8.
2. **Untested replay path: the same report retried after its lease ended** — `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/authority/completion.cljc:132-134` places the replay check (address equality) before `:ended-lease`, so the holder's retry after the release answers `:replayed` and writes nothing — correct idempotency, but `the-report-verifies-its-successor` (completion_test.cljc:308-311) pins only the *different*-successor `:ended-lease` branch. Severity: **nit** (coverage). Fix: one assertion after the `:policy` lapse — the same `succ-1` report answers `:replayed`, frames unchanged.
3. **Observation, no action: the fold accepts an edge placed after the epoch change within the same record** (`[lapse closure reclaimed edge]` folds clean; `edge-defect` reads only the lease entry and `::unedged`, ledger.cljc:708-715, 745-747). A record is atomic, so within-record order is cosmetic; the writer's canonical order (lapse, closure, edge, epoch) is pinned by exact `record-facts` assertions and a mutation. This matches C6's treatment of pairing (which is enforced) versus order (which is convention). Recorded so no one later mistakes the order for an unenforced invariant; no change requested.

## Rulings

- **The extra fold-level quarantine refusal (`closure-defect`'s `:quarantined-occurrence`, ledger.cljc:504-505): ACCEPT.** It cannot refuse any record the authority can write — the writer's `closure` already refuses quarantine (completion.cljc:199) — so it costs nothing legitimate; it fails closed on exactly the corrupt or foreign record reopen must not silently absorb, and it prevents an inconsistent closed-and-quarantined projection that C9's ancestry would then read. It is the fail-closed twin of `grant-defect`'s existing `:quarantined-occurrence`, it is tested (`the-fold-refuses-to-complete-a-quarantined-occurrence`) and mutation-killed. Keep it; no amendment.
- **Engineer's open question 7 (C9): ACCEPT the projection shape.** `:yin.k/closed {:dao.lease/lease l :yin.k/successor s | :yin.k/result r}` plus the offered baselines is the right ancestry input; C9 must treat a terminal closure (result, no successor) as the chain's end, and may reuse the public `successor-seen?` (ledger.cljc:455). Nothing to build in C8.
- **Q4, Q5, Q6: concur with the architect.** No separate reason — `:orphan` stands. No index — the O(occurrences) `successor-seen?` scan per report, completion and edge is a C12 cost note (doc debt item 8). `:dao.lease/released` at rebuild is step 5's meaning and is implemented and tested (grant.cljc:411-414, completion_test.cljc:618-623).
- **Report-time verification with no byte storage, and two predecessors reporting one successor: ACCEPT as built.** The reported address content-addresses the successor, so the eligible offer presents the same bytes the authority verified; the release-time re-checks (cause, live lease, quarantine, exhaustion, still-unseen) are the right minimal set. Two live reports naming one successor are unavoidable at report time; first completion wins the edge, the second release is a plain reclaim, and one-predecessor-per-successor still holds at the edge — tested at the authority and in the fold.
- **The engineer's remaining "for you" items are discharged or listed**: the commit exists (59479d9d); the UCF 7.7.2 table is doc debt (item 1 below); cljstyle/kondo/lanes are green on this tree per the brief, and my own column/ASCII/float checks pass.

